WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.760
- [Narrator] This vacant
parcel of land in Austin, Texas

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.490
might not look like much,
but it offers a glimpse

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.490
into how a skewed property
tax system is worsening

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.190
the housing crisis in
America's biggest cities.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.380
The lot has an annual property tax bill

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.560
of about $1.5 million per acre.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.680
But just six blocks over,
this luxury apartment building

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.170
is taxed at nearly double that rate.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.150
Cities all over the country are littered

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.430
with empty or sparsely built sites.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.220
Some economists say they
share a common factor,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.160
a tax system that
combines low taxes on land

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.170
with high taxes on buildings.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.240
Here's a closer look at that system,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.550
and the often overlooked role it plays

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.250
in the country's housing shortage.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.110
To understand the tax system,

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.930
let's head back to 701 Trinity Street.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.180
Located near the heart of downtown Austin,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.180
the 0.2 acre parcel is
valued at over $13.7 million.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.490
Yet for decades, the lot has
only been used for parking,

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.980
and the owners have paid
relatively little in taxes

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.170
because it doesn't contain any buildings.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.020
Cielo Property Group owns
the vacant plot of land.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.330
They did not respond to
a request for comment.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.290
- So traditional property
taxes perversely disincentivize

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.840
the kind of land use that we
want to see in our cities.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.300
When you're taxing
improvements, you're signaling

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.820
to the market that you
don't want improvements.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.920
- [Narrator] Josephine Faass
is the Executive Director

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.900
of the Robert Schalkenbach Foundation,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.760
a nonprofit research organization

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.220
that advocates for property tax reform.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.980
- So they actually affect
the way that people use land,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.590
or choose not to use
land, which is something

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.170
that might not initially
be, you know, obvious,

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.910
thinking about property taxes.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.920
- [Narrator] When landowners
improve their property,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.070
by adding a new room, for
instance, their taxes go up.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.410
In some cases, these taxes can be as much

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.990
as seven times higher for
a developed piece of land

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.000
compared to an empty lot.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.190
- The basic premise of
property taxes in America

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.350
is very simple, it's
basically the government

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.700
will try to figure out how much

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.170
is a piece of real estate worth,

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.420
and then based on that
value you'll be taxed.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.450
The more valuable your real
estate is, the higher your tax.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.250
- [Narrator] Presumably,
the owners of more

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.840
expensive buildings would be able

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.050
to afford more expensive
rates, but some economists say

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.630
the system has an unintended consequence.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.030
- It makes it easy for
people to take up properties

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.000
and then just hold them
until the value increases,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.470
sell at a profit, right?

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.920
So they'll buy low, sell high,
but without doing anything

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.650
in the interim, including
building housing.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.030
- [Narrator] Over the last few years,

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.490
Texas home prices have skyrocketed.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.050
In January, Austin's median
home price was $525,000,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.970
according to the Austin Board of Realtors.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:36.390
That's a 36% jump from January, 2020.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.250
- So housing shortages has always existed

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.320
in cities like New York and San Francisco.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.270
What's really changed
in the last couple years

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.830
is that you're seeing more of this issue

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.360
in places in the Sunbelt.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.100
I think Austin's really the textbook case

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.560
of the booming Sunbelt
city that's now running

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.780
into all these housing shortage problems,

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.330
'cause it used to be an
affordable place and now

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.180
it's really one of the most
expensive places in the South.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.250
- [Narrator] But despite
rising demand for housing,

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.980
developers struggle to
find sites to build on.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.890
- Developers paradoxically
are having a hard time

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.530
finding land, but even those
lots where law does allow you

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.900
to build, often people
just don't wanna sell.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.800
- [Narrator] Austin alone has
more than 17,500 vacant lots

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.470
zoned for residential use,
according to Altus Group,

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.240
a real estate analytics firm.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.600
In Atlanta, Georgia,
there's more than 13,000,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.290
and New York City has more than
77,000 lots that are vacant

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.380
or less than half the size
of what zoning allows.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.660
That's a lot of untapped potential.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.060
If the owners of all the
vacant lots in Austin

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.440
developed buildings to match

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.660
the current density of
the city, they could add

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.700
over 1 billion square feet of housing,

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.860
according to an Altus Group estimate

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.640
that's equivalent to about
384 Empire State buildings,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.730
or over 800,000 additional
apartment units.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.590
- So it's really adding to this issue

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:52.440
of affordable housing or
housing accessibility.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.360
- [Narrator] One solution
proposed by lawmakers

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.850
comes in the form of a Land Value Tax.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.160
This places a higher tax rate on land,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.350
while reducing Taxes on buildings.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.750
In theory, this system
would reduce the profit

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.630
that comes from owning a
vacant piece of property.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.360
- So if your tax code is set
up that you can make money

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.610
by improving the land that
you've just been sitting on,

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.710
or selling it to somebody who will,

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.210
that's what's gonna happen.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.610
- [Narrator] Research suggests
this type of taxing system

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:20.670
has increased building activity
in cities like Pittsburgh.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.050
- A couple of studies have actually shown

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:25.050
that they had more construction
activity in Pittsburgh,

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.270
relative to other nearby
and similar cities

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.280
that didn't have this sort of tax system.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.650
- [Narrator] Still, fairly
assessing the value of land

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.720
for taxation can be difficult.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.570
Some economists say it's
simpler to assess improvements

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.880
based on their income producing ability.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.440
There are also political hurdles

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.450
to implementing a new tax system.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.470
- One of the big challenges
in America is that often,

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:49.877
under state law, cities
and counties can't decide

00:04:49.877 --> 00:04:50.790
on their own tax system,

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.740
they basically have to defer to the state

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.570
- When it comes to building more housing,

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.000
some developers say a Land Value Tax

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.680
may only make a small difference.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.660
Factors like restrictive zoning laws

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.180
and rising material costs also play a role

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.950
in delaying construction projects.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:08.010
Despite the challenges, one city, Detroit,

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.350
may soon implement a Land Value Tax.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.200
If approved, lawmakers
and housing advocates say

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.770
this could be a crucial test
case for the tax policy.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.600
- Detroit would be the biggest city

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.270
in the history of America to
implement a Land Value Tax.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:22.860
And it'd be a fascinating test case

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:26.040
on whether this actually
works in the 21st century,

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:27.630
and whether others will follow suit.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.700
If it works out, it can
really change the way

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.124
Americans think about property taxes.

00:05:32.124 --> 00:05:34.874
(pleasant music)

